closest-possible
Same generator (the reference's random sequence and line templates) and the same recursive-descent parser, function for function, reading the text byte by byte; each run parses the whole text and adds the number of expressions and the hash of the last assignment's name. The reference's Node enum with Box children and String names is node arrays (children are indices, a name is a range of the text) reset at the start of every run, a bump allocator where the reference does one heap allocation per node (and frees the tree when the next run replaces it): this is the difference that makes it closest-possible. A guard statement at the top of each recursive function (`if g.pos < 0 { return 0; }`, never true) gives the compiler the return type it needs before the first recursive call. Stores are checked and undo-logged.
